feat: register the call log through the real right-dock API

The call-log tab never appeared because the services were read as plain
properties. `sidebarRightTabs` IS a real Client service — it is what
dsh-client-ui-sidebar-browser registers its own Browser tab type through:

    ctx.effect(() => ctx.sidebarRightTabs.register({
      id: BROWSER_ID, kind: BROWSER_KIND, multiple: true, priority: 'builtin',
      title: () => t('type.label'), ...
    }))

and the pane body plus its title are keyed by that definition's `id`:

    scope.slots.register({ name: 'sidebar.right.pane.tab',       key: BROWSER_ID }, BrowserBody)
    ctx.slots.register({   name: 'sidebar.right.pane.tab.title', key: BROWSER_ID }, BrowserTitle)

(An earlier note in this repo claimed the service did not exist; that grep ran
against a partial package set. It exists, and this is the shape.)

The two services are optional — they are absent when the right dock is not
mounted — so they are acquired with `ctx.inject(['sidebarRight',
'sidebarRightTabs'], scope => ...)`, the pattern dsh-client-ui-chat uses for the
same pair. The child scope activates when both appear and never activates when
they do not, so the tab is contributed on a shell that has a right dock and
silently skipped on one that does not. Reading either as a property throws
(Cordis resolves only injected services), which is what failed the whole web
boot.

Also registers the pane title, which the shipped Browser tab does and this
bundle did not — without it the tab had no label.

Verified live: `sidebar.right.pane.tab` now lists
`{ registrant: 'mf', key: 'dsh-plugin-mcp-manager', active: true }` beside the
shipped tabs (guide, documentpreview, terminal, files, browser, subagent,
deliverables, plan).

The test harness now models `ctx.inject` with Cordis's semantics and asserts
the tab type, the pane body and the title all register when the dock is present,
that nothing registers when it is absent, and that a rejected tab type costs the
tab rather than the boot.
